  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 311 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nSpeaking of the duration of the event, let's talk timeline: while \nareas east of the SGF CWA will start to see PoPs earlier, they begin \nto edge into the eastern reaches of our area Wednesday afternoon. Of \ncourse, temperatures will be in the upper 30s at first, so any \ninitial precipitation would fall as rain (assuming it makes it to \nthe ground). As the two aforementioned low pressures begin to \ninteract with each other, the Fujiwara effect will cause the \ncenter of the system to slowly pivot in place somewhere in the \nvicinity of Missouri (again, variation in low pressure center \nlocation among models). This will keep the high PoPs east \nthroughout Wednesday night. As we approach sunrise on \nThanksgiving, moisture finally begins wrapping around the \nbackside of the low center, and higher PoPs push into the \nnortheast. It will take some time before the system begins to \nlift itself out to the northeast, so a gradient in PoPs from 20%\nto 70% hangs out in the general area north of Hwy 60 and east \nof Hwy 65 for the majority of the day Thanksgiving Day. \nTemperatures will start out in the upper 20s/low 30s in the area\nof interest Thursday morning, but warm into the mid to upper \n30s late morning into the afternoon. This may result in a snow-\nto-rain transition at some point, or more likely a rain/snow \nmix. Thus, we have arrived at the final reason that snow \naccumulations are nearly impossible to determine.\n\nRegardless of what happens snow-wise, it is important to note that \nit will be very abnormally cold on Thanksgiving. Recall that normal \nhighs this time of year are in the low 50s, and most will struggle \nto break 40. Keep this in mind when traveling and planning any \noutdoor activities. Mercifully, Thursday night looks to be the last \nnight with widespread lows well below freezing. Heading into the \nweekend, ridging attempts to build in behind the departing trough, \nthough global models depict more of a zonal synoptic pattern. Either \nway, we should shift to southwesterly flow and decent WAA, helping \nto warm us back up to normal (and even a bit warmer) through the \nweekend.",
